From 8f962fcd68c83dd3d4b1574d41ac9cc8428f8d9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 08:55:58 +0000
Subject: software_isp: Add contrast autofocus

The simple pipeline handler had no autofocus at all: libcamera's AF
algorithms live only in the ipu3 and rpi IPAs, so on qcom-camss the
focus actuator was unreachable even though the kernel side was
complete (imx345 declares lens-focus = <&cam_vcm>, and camss creates
the ancillary link that CameraSensor turns into a CameraLens).

Port the AF stack from Pavel Machek's millicam_af_5 branch, whose
manual-focus foundation is on the list as patchwork 26241. The IPA
gains a sharpness statistic and an Af algorithm, and drives the lens
through a new lensControls argument to setSensorControls, which the
pipeline handler applies via CameraLens::setFocusPosition().

Rebased onto 0.7.2, which needed the per-line stats functions to take
the row number so the metric can be windowed vertically. Three fixes
on top of the original:

- SwStatsCpu::startFrame() and finishFrame() enumerate the fields they
  reset and merge, so sharpness had to be added to both. Without that
  the shared statistics buffer was never written and the IPA read a
  constant zero, leaving AF permanently idle.

- The packed formats compared a byte offset against a pixel width when
  windowing, so the horizontal window was wrong for them.

- The focus range was recorded before configure() clears the IPA
  context, so it was wiped and every focus position mapped onto zero.
  The lens then never moved despite the sweep converging.

Report sharpness as FocusFoM and the scan progress as AfState rather
than overloading AeState.

Verified on samsung-starqltechn (IMX345 + AK7375): the sweep
converges coarse-to-fine in ~3.5s and parks the actuator at the peak,
e.g. best focus 34.5 -> code 352, which agrees with a manual sweep of
focus_absolute that peaked at 384 on a different scene. AF re-runs by
itself when the scene changes, so it needs no app or PipeWire support.

The soft ISP autofocus added in 0015 self-triggers: Af::step() treats any
sharpness change of more than 30% as "focus lost" and restarts a sweep.
That is what makes it work in applications with no AF support at all, but
it also means a position set through LensPosition survives only until the
scene changes, so manual focus is not usable today.

Register controls::AfMode with AfModeManual and AfModeContinuous,
defaulting to continuous so existing behaviour is unchanged, and return
early from Af::step() in manual mode so nothing sweeps the lens away from
where the application put it.

Along the way:

  - Pass the focus lens ControlInfoMap to the IPA at init() time, not only
    at configure() time, so Af::init() can decline to register any focus
    control when the sensor has no actuator. Without this a fixed-focus
    sensor still advertises LensPosition and applications draw a focus
    slider that cannot move anything.

  - Fix the AfTrigger polarity. AfTriggerStart is 0 and AfTriggerCancel is
    1, but the code started a sweep on 1 and stopped one on 0. Advertise
    the control as an enum of the two values rather than a 0..1 range so
    that applications, and the PipeWire SPA bridge, present it as a
    choice.

  - Report AfMode in the metadata, and report AfStateIdle rather than
    AfStateFocused while in manual mode, as the specification requires.

  - Drop the const from the CameraSensor the SoftwareIsp constructor
    takes, as CameraSensor::focusLens() is not const. The only caller
    already holds a non-const sensor.

  - Express LensPosition in dioptres, as the control specifies, instead of
    on an invented 0..100 scale. An application that follows the spec and
    asks for 0.5 to focus at 2m was previously served a position five
    codes off the bottom of the actuator's travel, indistinguishable from
    infinity; it now gets what it asked for. Positions are dioptres
    throughout the algorithm, including the sweep, and are converted to
    actuator codes in one place, through a piecewise-linear map. The
    sweep's step sizes become fractions of the focus range rather than
    the hardcoded 2 and 0.2, which keeps the coarse-to-fine search
    identical in shape whatever the range is.

    The map is read from the sensor's tuning file using libipa's Pwl and
    the same flat x,y list schema as the Raspberry Pi IPA's "rpi.af" map,
    so a calibration measured for one can be transcribed to the other:

        - Af:
            map: [ 0.0, 100, 10.0, 1000 ]
            range: { min: 0.0, max: 10.0, default: 1.0 }

    No tuning file carries a map yet, so the code falls back to assuming
    the actuator spans infinity to 10cm linearly and logs a warning that
    reported distances are not trustworthy. That fallback reproduces the
    previous behaviour exactly, just relabelled; making it correct needs
    a bench calibration of the module, which is a physical measurement
    rather than a code change.

  - Offset the lens position by focus_min when mapping it onto the
    actuator range. This is a no-op for the ak7375 on starqltechn, whose
    range starts at 0, but it is wrong for any actuator that does not.

CameraSensor opens the lens subdevice when the camera is created and
keeps it open for the camera manager's lifetime, so with a long-lived
client such as WirePlumber a VCM driver like dw9719 never
runtime-suspends. Whatever position autofocus last set is held with the
coil energised until the next capture: on the Moto G Play (2024) the
lens stayed at code 552 of 1023 after a capture, about half the
actuator's full-scale current, indefinitely.

Move the lens to its minimum position in stopDevice(), after the
software ISP (and with it the IPA) has stopped and can no longer move
it. Ramp down from the last position set, 16 codes a millisecond as
dw9719's own suspend does, rather than jumping: with no coil current at
the bottom of travel, a lens dropped from mid-travel lands on its end
stop with an audible click.

The software ISP's autofocus swept the full range, infinity to the near
limit in 2% steps and then finer around the best point, for every focus
acquisition, and treated any sharpness change of 30% in either direction
as focus lost, which restarted that sweep. Each move waited 10 frames.
Refocusing after a small change of subject distance took seconds and
visibly racked the lens end to end.

Hill-climb instead. When sharpness stays 20% below the reference for 3
frames, probe one small step either side of the current position:
  - one side better: climb that way, growing the step while sharpness
    rises, then refine around the best position with two-sided probes
    at half the step each round;
  - both sides worse: the lens is still on the peak (the scene or the
    exposure changed), so hold;
  - no difference: the lens is on the flat far out of focus, so probe
    wider, and only then fall back to the old sweep.
First focus and AfTrigger start with the wide probe from the current
position. A rise in sharpness only raises the reference. Frames are
skipped after a move only for the requests already in flight (3), or 5
after a large move, and each measurement averages two frames.

First focus probed a small step either side of the default position and
climbed from there. Far from focus the sharpness curve is broad and
nearly flat, so the climb often stopped at the end of travel on a
defocused image and reported it as focused, and a flat probe fell back
to the old full sweep, which took twelve seconds at 30 fps.

Acquire focus, and handle AfTrigger and a flat probe, with a coarse scan
instead: nine evenly spaced positions, one frame each after the in-flight
requests clear, starting from the end of travel nearer the lens. The
scan stops as soon as sharpness has fallen 40% below the best point two
or more points back, and the best point is then refined with the
existing two-sided probes. Focus acquires in about two seconds.

Also require five frames below the reference, not three, before focus
counts as lost, so hand shake while framing restarts the search less.

Continuous AF treated any sustained sharpness drop as focus lost and
searched at once. Contrast cannot tell defocus from motion blur or an
exposure step, so while framing by hand the lens breathed constantly,
and the reference ratcheted up to any single sharp frame, making the
next ordinary frames look like a 20% drop.

Do what contrast continuous-AF implementations generally do: keep a
scene signature (the normalised luma histogram and exposure x gain), let
a sustained drop or a changed scene only arm a refocus, and search once
the signature has held still for ten frames. Sharpness recovering while
waiting disarms it. For a second after focusing only a big drop counts,
and the reference follows drift symmetrically and only while the scene
is still.

Signed-off-by: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
